  "query_preview": "..\nFriday: In the wake of Thursday's cold front, temperatures\nFriday will cool by 8-12 degrees, returning to below average in\nthe upper 60s to mid 70s. For most of the region, Friday will be\ndry as the Inland Northwest sits in between upper-level systems.\nThe northern ID Panhandle will be the one exception with a\n15-30% chance of showers and isolated thunderstorms lingering \ninto Friday afternoon. Instability looks rather weak again \ntomorrow (100-200 J/kg), so widespread lightning is not \nexpected.\n\nSaturday and Sunday: A significant pattern change arrives \nSaturday afternoon as a deep trough begins tracking inland from\noff the coast. Saturday morning and early afternoon is looking\npleasant with highs ranging from the upper 60s to upper 70s. \nWinds will be breezy out of the north down the Okanogan Valley \nand out of the northeast across the Columbia Basin with gusts of\n25 to 35 mph. Ensembles depict a swath of moisture moving up\nfrom the south Saturday afternoon into Sunday with PWATs\nincreasing to 150% of normal. Precipitation chances will start\nto move in Saturday evening as the atmosphere saturates. By\nSunday morning, there will be a 60-90% chance of rain\nregionwide.\n\nThere is high confidence that most locations will see \nmeasurable precip, but significant uncertainty remains regarding\ntotal precip amounts. Overall, rainfall amounts are trending\nhigher with each model run, but there are still substantial \ndifferences between the high-end and low-end amounts predicted\nby the models. For example, the 10th percentile (low-end) rain\namount forecast for Spokane is just 0.1 inches, while the 90th\npercentile (high-end) amount is 1.4 inches! \n\nAs mentioned in the previous forecast discussion, the chances\ncontinue to trend higher for 0.5 inches or more of rain from\nSaturday evening through Monday morning. The NBM's latest\nchances for at least 0.5 inches of precip are as follows:\n\n- 45-60% for Central WA\n- 60-80% for Eastern WA\n- 75-85% for ID Panhandle\n\nTemperatures Sunday will be well below normal in the 50s and \n60s. \n\nMonday through Thursday: High pressure builds into the region as\nthe weekend system exits to the east. Temperatures will \ngradually return to near seasonal normals in the mid to upper \n70s by late next week with dry conditions expected. Fog\npotential will be something to monitor Monday morning with a\nmoist boundary layer following all of the weekend rain. /Fewkes",
